<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy — privacy manifest for the tunnel extension.

  A second file rather than a second section of the app's, because the manifest
  is per-BUNDLE and Apple scans each binary it finds. The extension links
  libwg-go.a, which the app binary does not, and that alone gives it a
  different answer to give.

  See App/Resources/PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy for the app's, and for the editing
  rules that apply to both.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
  <false/>
  <key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
  <array/>

  <!--
    Empty, and that is the whole point of the product.

    This process moves the user's packets and reports nothing about them to us.
    It reads its configuration out of the saved VPN profile the system hands it
    (see PacketTunnelProvider.startTunnel), answers the app's own
    handleAppMessage with the backend's runtime counters, and transmits to no
    one else. There is no analytics channel, no crash reporter and no log
    upload — os.Logger writes to the device's log, which never leaves it.
  -->
  <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
  <array/>

  <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
  <array>
    <!--
      Not our Swift code — the Go runtime inside libwg-go.a. wireguard-go keeps
      its handshake, rekey and keepalive timers on a monotonic clock, and on
      darwin the Go runtime's nanotime is mach_absolute_time, imported
      dynamically from libSystem. Apple's scanner reads the symbol, not the
      intent, so an undeclared extension earns ITMS-91053 even though no line
      in Tunnel/Sources asks for the boot time.

      35F9.1 is the accurate reason of the four: elapsed time between events
      inside this process. It is NOT 8FFB.1 (absolute timestamps for user-
      visible events) and NOT 3D61.1 (CoreMotion), neither of which happens
      here.

      ⚠️ Do not delete this on the grounds that grepping Tunnel/Sources finds
      nothing. It is declared for a dependency's binary, and it goes away only
      if wireguard-go does.
    -->
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategorySystemBootTime</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
      <array>
        <string>35F9.1</string>
      </array>
    </dict>
  </array>
</dict>
</plist>
